re identical.","A one-time visit is automatically treated as a fixed repeated social setting."],"answer":["B"]},{"id":51,"category":"Social Drinking","type":"single","prompt":"A Jew is asked to have a business drink at a non-Jewish bar. Which statement matches the course positions?","choices":["S”A/Gra permit an ordinary business beer, while Rav Moshe and the Rama prohibit any drink in a non-Jewish bar.","S”A/Gra and the Rama all prohibit; Rav Moshe permits whenever the meeting has a business purpose, even without איבה.","S”A/Gra baseline prohibits; Rav Moshe allows room where refusal creates איבה; Ohr L’Tzion remains cautious; the Rama permits ordinary beer. A Jewish-owned bar is outside this specific non-Jewish-establishment gezeirah.","S”A/Gra baseline permits a business drink, while the Rama permits only in a Jewish-owned bar."],"answer":["C"]},{"id":52,"category":"Wine Contact & Touch","type":"single","prompt":"Which set of statements about who touched the wine matches the course framework?","choices":["Muslim: drinking concern but benefit permitted, with Rav Ovadya lenient in doubtful touch; public Shabbat desecrator: drinking can be forbidden but benefit permitted; anti-religious/hostile violator: all relevant views prohibit drinking; benefit from wine touched by a nonreligious Jew: permitted.","Muslim: drinking and benefit prohibited; public Shabbat desecrator: drinking permitted but benefit prohibited; anti-religious/hostile violator: drinking permitted; benefit from wine touched by a nonreligious Jew: prohibited.","Muslim: fully permitted even after deliberate contact; public Shabbat desecrator: drinking and benefit prohibited; anti-religious/hostile violator: benefit prohibited but drinking permitted; benefit from wine touched by a nonreligious Jew: prohibited.","Muslim: benefit prohibited only in doubtful touch; public Shabbat desecrator: always permitted; anti-religious/hostile violator: same as traditional identity; benefit from wine touched by a nonreligious Jew: prohibited."],"answer":["A"]},{"id":53,"category":"Wine Contact & Touch","type":"single","prompt":"Which list gives the four course conditions for full touch prohibition?","choices":["Ownership, color, temperature, and duration.","Intent, ownership, benefit, and whether the bottle is sealed.","Knowledge, benefit, pouring, and a non-Jewish owner.","Intent to touch; knowledge that it is wine; purpose directed at the wine rather than incidental/preoccupied contact; and shaking/agitation."],"answer":["D"]},{"id":54,"category":"Wine Contact & Touch","type":"single","prompt":"Compare two acts by a non-Jew. Case 1: the person pours the wine without full shaking. Case 2: the person shakes an open bottle without lifting it. Which answer matches the course S”A/Rama framework?","choices":["Case 1 is fully permitted under S”A; the Rama forbids it. Case 2 is permitted according to both.","Case 1 is prohibited for both drinking and benefit under S”A; the Rama never changes that. Case 2 is prohibited only for drinking under S”A.","Case 1: S”A prohibits drinking but permits benefit; the Rama’s downgrade can permit drinking, with Shach emphasizing practical reliance especially for loss. Case 2: S”A prohibits both drinking and benefit; the Rama permits on his downgrade, with course practice recording reliance particularly in loss.","Case 1 and Case 2 have identical status under every course view."],"answer":["C"]},{"id":55,"category":"Wine Contact & Touch","type":"multi","prompt":"Select all that apply. Which statements match the course practical contact rules?","choices":["Rav Ovadya treats a Muslim’s deliberate pour as still forbidding drinking.","Rav Elyashiv treats a nonreligious Jew’s pour as significant because pouring can function like shaking.","Clinking glasses is permitted because the contact is indirect through the vessel, the act is not idolatrous, and the wine is not handled in the prohibited manner.","For accidental touch, S”A prohibits drinking but permits benefit; the Rama can permit drinking/benefit for today’s non-Jews, while Shach is more cautious without loss.","Shaking/lifting a closed bottle is permitted under both S”A and Rama in the taught matrix.","A Muslim’s deliberate pour is automatically permitted because a Muslim is not an idolater.","Accidental touch is more severe than deliberate pouring under every course view."],"answer":["A","B","C","D","E"]},{"id":56,"category":"Wine Contact & Touch","type":"single","prompt":"Mevushal kosher wine is poured in a continuous stream into a cup that contains already-forbidden non-mevushal wine residue. The source bottle represents a genuine great loss. Which statement best matches the course נצוק rules?","choices":["Because the source is מבושל, the forbidden residue is erased and there is no possible נצוק issue.","The source bottle is always prohibited, even in a genuine great-loss case.","The source can still face a נצוק problem because מבושל does not erase already-forbidden receiving residue, but the course framework allows reliance on the non-connection view in a genuine great-loss case.","נצוק applies only when the receiving cup contains kosher wine."],"answer":["C"]},{"id":57,"category":"Unattended Wine","type":"single","prompt":"Which statement correctly combines the course unattended-wine rules?","choices":["Open wine left alone with an idolater is prohibited immediately under S”A when the access conditions are met; open wine left with a Muslim is prohibited after enough time to walk a mil under the course rule, with Shach potentially stricter where drinking itself is the concern; a fixed known absence schedule undermines יוצא ונכנס.","An idolater and a Muslim have the same waiting period, and a fixed weekly schedule strengthens יוצא ונכנס.","Open wine left with an idolater is protected until enough time to open and reseal the bottle, while Muslim access prohibits it immediately.","יוצא ונכנס depends only on whether the bottle is מבושל."],"answer":["A"]},{"id":58,"category":"Unattended Wine","type":"single","prompt":"Which statement best matches the course protections for wine left with a non-Jew?","choices":["יוצא ונכנס works even when the non-Jew knows the full absence window and the area is locked against your return; if protection fails, closed wine is prohibited immediately while open wine gets extra time; cameras help only when hidden.","For יוצא ונכנס, the non-Jew must not know you will be gone a meaningful time, must not see you approaching, and the area must not be locked against your return. If protection fails, open wine can be prohibited immediately while closed wine requires time to open and reseal undetected. Protections include effective seals/locks, known monitoring or fear of being caught, hidden wine, and the taught effectiveness of a screw-on metal cap.","יוצא ונכנס requires a fixed schedule so the non-Jew knows when you return; if protection fails, open and closed wine have the same timing; employment consequences and cam